  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Showers and thunderstorms develop tonight through \nMonday, with locally heavy rainfall possible.\n\nLatest guidance continues to suggest the eastward shift of the large \nscale upper level trough during the second half of the weekend into \nearly next week. The upper trough looks to deepen to the point of \nclosing off over the Ohio Valley and mid-Atlantic states sometime in \nthe Monday-Tuesday time frame. A wavy cold front will slowly push \neastward across southern Ontario and then across our region between\nSunday and the first part of Monday, with a broad surface low \nforming underneath the closed low early next week. \n\nA deep south-southwesterly flow will develop out ahead of the wavy \ncold front and spread a plume of rich Gulf-based moisture across our \nregion, with PWATs increasing to near 2 inches. Within this \nenvironment, warm advection/isentropic ascent will result in \nincreasing chances for showers and thunderstorms tonight and Sunday,\nwith this further aided during Sunday by the approach of the \nfront/trough axis, increasing low level convergence along the nose\nof a 30-40 knot low level jet, and the development of weak to \nmodest diurnally-driven instability.\n\nGiven the high atmospheric moisture content and near-parallel nature \nof the low-mid level flow to the approaching front and trough axis, \nthe potential will exist for storms with heavy rainfall to develop \nand train out ahead of/along the approaching frontal boundary Sunday \ninto Sunday night, with the potential for localized flash flooding. \nWhile a decent amount of deep-layer shear (30-40 knots) does look to \ndevelop across far western New york Sunday and then linger through\nSunday night, the presence of only weak to modest instability \ncurrently looks to largely preclude much of an organized severe \nweather threat. \n\nShowers and a few thunderstorms could lingering Monday, especially \nalong eastern and southern portions of the area depending on the \nspeed of the cold frontal passage.",
